1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
Hi, I'm Dave from the Burp Scanner development team and today I'm going to talk a little bit

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:11,280
about insertion points in Burp Scanner. So insertion points have always been a source

3
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:17,280
of confusion for our users because it's not always been clear within a request what is Burp Scanner

4
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:21,560
targeting and what is the status of the insertion points that are being scanned.

5
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:27,060
And one of the reasons for this is that Burp Scanner hasn't always been the most visible in

6
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:33,160
terms of what is going on under the hood. In older releases of Burp Suite such as the one I have here

7
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:38,220
you could only ever see a count of the insertion points that were present in any given request.

8
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:43,420
You couldn't click on the request and see what the insertion points were and actually the insertion

9
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:48,740
point count itself was only populated when the item started being actively scanned. So for these

10
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:52,800
requests that haven't yet started you didn't get a count of the insertion points. You couldn't see

11
00:00:52,800 --> 00:01:00,080
what was still to come. In Burp Suite 2020 4.1 we introduced the insertion point panel to help show

12
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:06,000
what parts of a request are being attacked by Burp Scanner and in particular what the status of those

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,840
insertion points are. So in Burp Suite 2020 4.1 you can see that the insertion point count is populated for

14
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:19,760
all of the items and every time you click on an item you have the option of either looking at the base

15
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:25,120
response or the new insertion point panel. You can also right click on a request and show insertion

16
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:31,280
points to make the panel appear if it's not already showing. And in this insertion points panel you can

17
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:38,000
see everything that Burp Scanner identified as being potentially worthy of attacking as an insertion point.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,200
So in this request you can see we have URL parameters, we have cookie parameters, we have header parameters

19
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:49,120
and folder and file name parameters as part of the request. There is also the opportunity to see how

20
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:54,160
Burp Scanner can take insertion points from one location and put them in another location. So for

21
00:01:54,160 --> 00:02:00,080
instance in this request you can see that we changed the parameter from being in the URL to being a body

22
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:05,680
parameter. Now Burp Scanner doesn't scan these types of insertion points by default but you can configure it

23
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:10,000
to do so and this was just a way of helping to surface that capability within the scanner.

24
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,760
Another really cool thing about the insertion point view is that when you click on the insertion points

25
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:20,720
themselves you get a request highlight and the request will jump to the insertion point to show

26
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:27,120
it you in your request response viewer. So you can always see exactly what Burp Scanner is trying to

27
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:34,320
attack in the request. You also get a status icon on the right side of the panel which indicates whether

28
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:38,960
Burp has started auditing the insertion point or it should change from pending to auditing to

29
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:46,320
audited when it's complete. And you can also see that in some cases such as this cookie which is

30
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:50,240
present on Gin and Juice Shop which supports we use deliberately vulnerable web application.

31
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:59,680
This insertion point has a base value which is base64 encoded and when you base64 decode that value

32
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:06,720
it reveals that there are a few more insertion points for us to attack. My colleague Tom already did a

33
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:13,440
video about nested insertion points within Burp Scanner and how we can attack insertion points that

34
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:18,960
are within certain encodings and make sure that the payloads are in the right encoding type for the

35
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:24,880
for the server to understand. But now we actually have a visual representation where you can click through

36
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:31,360
each of the insertion points and see whether after decoding there was further insertion points underneath it.

37
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:37,920
This is a really big step forward for showing all of our users what the scanner is actually up to.

38
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:44,720
We continue to try and make updates that help understand what the scanner is doing, how the scanner

39
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:58,320
is working. So please give it a go and let us know if you have any feedback.

